  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Noble Supply and Logistics has $334M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Noble Supply and Logistics won 106,729 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $3.1K. Noble Supply and Logistics obligations are down 65.3% year over year. Historical obligations show growth through FY24, then a decline: FY21 $292M, FY22 $784M, FY23 $1.1B, FY24 $1.2B, FY25 $962M, FY26 $334M. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer in FY2026 to date, followed by the General Services Administration and several civilian agencies. Industry exposure is concentrated in a handful of NAICS markets, with Service Establishment Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers, All Other Miscellaneous Manufacturing, and Bolt, Nut, Screw, Rivet, and Washer Manufacturing accounting for the largest shares of obligated dollars. This profile shows a contractor with large-scale historical federal business, a high award count of mostly small-dollar transactions in the current year, and a strong reliance on DoD demand in the current fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Noble Supply and Logistics' federal sales?",
        "body": "Noble Supply and Logistics has a high award count and small average awards: 106,729 awards and an average award size of $3.1K. Noble Supply and Logistics total obligations for FY2026 to date are $334M, and obligations are down 65.3% year over year. The Department of Defense accounts for $300M of FY2026 to date obligations, which shows heavy buyer concentration. The General Services Administration accounts for $32.5M and other agencies are much smaller buyers."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets and agencies drive the company's FY2026 obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $300M in obligations for FY2026 to date, followed by the General Services Administration with $32.5M. Service Establishment Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers is the largest NAICS market at $107M, followed by All Other Miscellaneous Manufacturing at $83.3M and Bolt, Nut, Screw, Rivet, and Washer Manufacturing at $51.5M. Several smaller NAICS markets and civilian agencies provide modest additional obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
